    "profile": "TITLE: Photosynthesis: Inputs, Light Capture, and Outputs\n\nINTRO: Nothing here is scored. A wrong answer only shows what to look at next. This lesson traces how a leaf turns light into sugar.\n\nSECTION 1 - The inputs and the energy source\nPlants make glucose, a sugar, from carbon dioxide and water. They use light energy from the sun to drive this reaction. The word equation is: carbon dioxide + water -> glucose + oxygen. Takeaway: carbon dioxide and water are the raw materials; sunlight supplies the energy.\nEXAMPLE: Try it: In a basketball game, the players who receive the pass are the inputs. Which two inputs does a leaf receive? Answer: carbon dioxide and water.\n\nSECTION 2 - Where light is captured\nChlorophyll is the green pigment that absorbs light. It sits inside chloroplasts, which are small compartments in leaf cells. Most photosynthesis happens in the leaves because leaves hold many chloroplasts. Takeaway: chlorophyll in leaf chloroplasts catches the light.\nEXAMPLE: Arrow chain: sunlight -> chlorophyll in chloroplasts -> glucose production. Try it: A comic artist's ink sits in a pen; chlorophyll sits in which leaf structure? Answer: the chloroplast.\n\nSECTION 3 - The outputs\nThe plant uses the captured light energy to build glucose from carbon dioxide and water. Oxygen is released as a by-product of this reaction. Glucose stores energy and supports growth. Takeaway: photosynthesis makes glucose and releases oxygen.\nEXAMPLE: Try it: What gas is released as a by-product of photosynthesis? Answer: oxygen.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: Trace a leaf's photosynthesis from inputs to outputs.\n  1. Start with the raw materials: carbon dioxide enters from the air, and water enters from the soil. Reason: these are the inputs the plant builds glucose from.\n  2. Add the energy: sunlight is absorbed by chlorophyll in the chloroplasts. Reason: light energy powers the reaction.\n  3. Build the sugar: the plant uses that energy to make glucose from carbon dioxide and water. Reason: glucose is the useful sugar product.\n  4. Release the by-product: oxygen leaves the leaf. Reason: oxygen is not the goal product here; it is released.\n  ANSWER: carbon dioxide + water + light energy -> glucose + oxygen, with chlorophyll in chloroplasts absorbing the light.\n\nCOMMON MISTAKES:\nWrong: Plants take in oxygen and release carbon dioxide during photosynthesis. Why: this reverses the gases; carbon dioxide is a raw material taken in, and oxygen is the by-product released. Right: carbon dioxide + water -> glucose + oxygen, so carbon dioxide goes in and oxygen comes out.\n\nSUMMARY: Plants make glucose from carbon dioxide and water, using sunlight absorbed by chlorophyll in leaf chloroplasts; oxygen is released.\n\nCHECK QUESTIONS:\n1. From memory, which pair are the raw materials for photosynthesis? [choices: Carbon dioxide and water | Oxygen and glucose | Carbon dioxide and oxygen | Water and glucose] -> Carbon dioxide and water\n2. From memory, where in a leaf cell does chlorophyll absorb light? [typed answer] -> In the chloroplasts",
    "mirror": "TITLE: Photosynthesis: How a Leaf Makes Its Own Food\n\nINTRO: A plant cannot go and get a sandwich. It makes its own food inside its leaves. That job is called photosynthesis: making glucose, a sugar, from carbon dioxide and water, powered by light energy.\n\nSECTION 1 - The recipe: what goes in, what comes out\nThe rule: a plant makes its own food, a sugar called glucose. It builds glucose from two ingredients: carbon dioxide, a gas in the air, and water, which the roots pull up from the soil. Light energy from the sun is the power that makes the building happen. Light is not an ingredient that turns into sugar; it is the power that drives the joining.\nEXAMPLE: Here is the same rule as a picture you can read left to right:\n\ncarbon dioxide (from the air)\n+ water (from the soil)\n-> powered by light energy from the sun ->\nglucose (the plant's food, a sugar) + oxygen (released as a by-product)\n\nRead the top line as the ingredients and the bottom line as what comes out.\n\nSECTION 2 - Where it happens: leaves, chloroplasts, chlorophyll\nPhotosynthesis happens mainly in the leaves, the plant's food factories. Inside leaf cells are tiny green packets called chloroplasts. Chloroplasts hold chlorophyll, the green material that absorbs light energy. That is why leaves look green, and why a plant in a dark cupboard makes no sugar even with plenty of water and air.\nEXAMPLE: Think of a basketball team. The players do the scoring, and the court is where the game happens, not the car park. In a leaf, the chloroplasts are the players: they hold chlorophyll, which catches the light, and that is where the glucose gets built. Cells with no chloroplasts, like the ones in a white root, catch no light at all.\n\nSECTION 3 - Oxygen: the leftover\nOxygen is a by-product: something made along the way, not the thing the plant is working to make. The plant lets that oxygen out into the air through tiny holes in its leaves. That released oxygen is the same gas you breathe in. So some of every breath you take came out of a leaf.\nEXAMPLE: In a comic panel you could draw a plant on a windowsill with a fat arrow going into a leaf labelled carbon dioxide, a sun in the corner, and an arrow coming out labelled oxygen. One picture shows the whole idea: gas in, light powering the leaf, gas out.\n\nTry it: A plant has water and air but sits in a dark room. Name the one thing it is missing that stops it making glucose. Answer: light energy from the sun.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: A comic artist draws a sunflower on a windowsill. Explain, step by step, how that plant makes its food. Use the words light energy, chlorophyll, chloroplasts, carbon dioxide, water, glucose and oxygen.\n  1. Step 1. The sun shines on the leaf. Light energy from the sun lands on the leaf surface. Nothing has been made yet; this is only the power arriving.\n  2. Step 2. Inside the leaf cells, chloroplasts hold chlorophyll. Chlorophyll is green and absorbs the light energy. This is why the light has to land on a leaf, where the chloroplasts are.\n  3. Step 3. Water arrives from the roots. The roots pull water out of the soil and it travels up the plant to the leaves. Water is ingredient one.\n  4. Step 4. Carbon dioxide arrives from the air. The gas moves into the leaf through tiny holes. Carbon dioxide is ingredient two.\n  5. Step 5. The light energy powers the joining of carbon dioxide and water into glucose. Glucose is the sugar the plant uses as food, and making it is the whole point of the job.\n  6. Step 6. Oxygen is left over, so the plant releases it into the air. Oxygen is the by-product, not the goal.\n  ANSWER: Carbon dioxide plus water, powered by light energy that chlorophyll in the chloroplasts absorbed, makes glucose, and oxygen is released as a by-product. This happens mainly in the leaves.\n\nCOMMON MISTAKES:\nWrong: Plants get their food from the soil. Why: the soil supplies water, not sugar; a plant in damp soil but in the dark still cannot make glucose. Right: the leaf builds glucose from carbon dioxide and water, powered by light energy.\nWrong: A plant breathes in oxygen and lets out carbon dioxide, so that is photosynthesis. Why: that is the plant using up its food for energy, which is a different job; photosynthesis runs the other way round. Right: in photosynthesis carbon dioxide goes in and oxygen comes out as a by-product.\nWrong: Photosynthesis happens all over the plant, so a root makes just as much glucose as a leaf. Why: the reaction needs chlorophyll, which sits in the chloroplasts, and those are mainly in the leaves. Right: photosynthesis happens mainly in the leaves, where the light is caught.\n\nSUMMARY: Plants make glucose, a sugar, from carbon dioxide and water. Light energy from the sun powers that joining. Chlorophyll, inside the chloroplasts of leaf cells, absorbs the light. Oxygen is released as a by-product, and the whole job happens mainly in the leaves.\n\nCHECK QUESTIONS:\n1. Which gas does a plant take in from the air to make glucose? Hint: picture the arrow going into the leaf. [choices: Oxygen | Carbon dioxide | Nitrogen | Water vapour] -> Carbon dioxide\n2. What is the name of the green material inside a leaf that absorbs light energy? Hint: it sits inside the chloroplasts. [typed answer] -> chlorophyll\n3. A plant sits in a dark cupboard with damp soil and plenty of air. What happens to its glucose making, and why? Hint: check which ingredient comes from the sun. [choices: It carries on as normal, because soil and air are all it needs | It stops, because there is no light energy to power the joining of carbon dioxide and water | It slows down but keeps going, because chlorophyll still works in the dark | It stops, because carbon dioxide cannot get into a leaf in the dark] -> It stops, because there is no light energy to power the joining of carbon dioxide and water"
   }
  }
